            'everyday' => 'In a crowd you\'re usually the observer — not many words, every detail retained. The slight remove makes you unusually perceptive about people, and it makes those close to you occasionally need to check: are you actually in this?',
            'deep' => 'Watching is participation, not absence — but that\'s hard for a partner to grasp. They can easily feel spectated, scored, even inspected for flaws. So say it in your own voice: I am never more engaged than when I\'m watching you. Remember too that someone willing to be watched is trusting you — say that to them and they\'ll go further. And if you record: whose phone it lives on, and when it gets deleted, is settled before the first frame. That trust is worth more than the footage.',
            'strength' => 'You know exactly where your switch is, and you don\'t drift with the crowd — the position you enjoy is precisely defined. Self-knowledge like that is rarer than any technique.',
            'watch' => '"Watching" is easily read as spectating, scoring, checking out. They need to hear it from your mouth: when I\'m watching you is when I\'m most in it.',
            'match' => 'Made for the "Exhibitionist" — you want to watch, they want to be watched; one look closes the loop. The most direct pairing of all 20.',
            'friction' => 'Go slow with the shy and the privacy-minded: being watched is pressure for them, not pleasure. Start with a mirror, or one small stretch they\'ve okayed — don\'t rush the escalation.',
            'partner_line' => '"When I watch you, I am at my most engaged — not spectating, and definitely not scoring. You letting go in front of my eyes is exactly the participation I want."',
        ],
        'exhib' => [
            'name' => 'Exhibitionist', 'slug' => 'exhibitionist', 'tag' => 'Being watched amplifies',
            'line' => 'Being watched amplifies everything. With an audience, you actually open up more.',
            'long' => 'Being looked at gives every move you make its weight. You don\'t necessarily need anyone physically there — knowing they\'re watching is enough: lights on, facing the mirror, told to perform for them, you\'re far bolder than usual.',
            'signals' => [
                'Knowing they\'re watching changes every single move you make.',
                'Curtains not quite shut, door unlocked — and you\'re more turned on, not less.',
                'Told "show me how you touch yourself," you throw yourself into it.',
                'Watched by someone you like, you open up; watched by someone uncertain, you shrink.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'Being watched amplifies every reaction you have: their eyes fixed on you, being told to touch yourself for them, a mirror alongside, a camera pointed — you\'re far bolder than usual and far louder too. It\'s actually a preference that runs on safety — the audience must be one you chose: watched by someone you like, the pleasure doubles; watched by someone you\'re unsure of, the anxiety does. The gap is enormous. Starting with a mirror, or with just-the-two-of-you footage deleted right after, is far safer than actually adding people.',
            'likes' => [
                'Being told "show me how you do it" — they may look, not touch.',
                'Facing the mirror, facing the camera (kept strictly between you two).',
                'Lights on, watched from start to finish.',
                'That tension of possibly being caught — kept within legal bounds, never anywhere an uninvolved person could actually see you.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'You light up under attention: on stage, in photos, when praised, you\'re a different person than usual. It isn\'t showing off — being properly seen is fuel for you.',
            'deep' => 'Being watched gives your movements weight, and that\'s a preference that quietly requires safety — the audience has to be one you picked. So keep the two cases sharply apart: watched by someone you like versus someone you\'re unsure of. The first amplifies pleasure, the second amplifies anxiety, and nearly every next-day regret comes from the second. Starting from a mirror, from one sentence — "I\'m watching" — is much safer than adding people. And that delicious risk of being caught stays within legal lines: being seen by an uninvolved stranger isn\'t play, it\'s dragging someone else in without their consent.',
            'strength' => 'Watched, every move you make carries weight — you convert being admired into pure energy and go further than you ever would alone. It\'s a magnetic kind of confidence.',
            'watch' => 'This preference needs safety more than it looks: the audience must be yours to choose. A loving gaze amplifies pleasure; an uncertain one amplifies anxiety. The two are worlds apart.',
            'match' => 'A perfect closed loop with the "Voyeur" — they want to watch, you want to be watched. Strong with the "Dominant" too: eyes on you from start to finish, your urge to perform finally has its audience.',
            'friction' => 'Mind the privacy-first "Guardian": your "watch me" can land on their boundary. Try mirrors and strictly-between-you-two versions first; discuss the rest later.',
            'partner_line' => '"Being watched by you opens me right up — but who\'s watching matters. It\'s your gaze I want; that\'s what makes me feel bold. Not just anyone\'s."',
        ],
        'explorer' => [
            'name' => 'Explorer', 'slug' => 'explorer', 'tag' => 'Always a next thing to try',
            'line' => 'Haven\'t tried it? Try it first, judge later. The list always has a next item.',
            'long' => 'You have zero resistance to the words "not yet tried" — new position, new toy, new location. Repetition makes you drift; novelty lights you up head to toe. Being with you is never boring — but they do have to keep up.',
            'signals' => [
                'If it\'s untried, your default is: try first, judge later.',
                'Your list always has a next item — a toy, a location, a scenario.',
                'Too many repeats of the same thing and your mind starts wandering.',
                'You propose things unprompted, and rejection doesn\'t scare you much.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'Your drive in bed comes from "not yet tried": the toy you haven\'t used, the position you haven\'t done, the place you\'ve never done it, the spots you don\'t normally touch. Being with you is genuinely hard to get bored of — but watch that exploring doesn\'t turn into a to-do list: always racing to the next item means never fully living a single one, until even orgasms become checkboxes. Repeat a favorite occasionally — repetition grows new things too. And a partner lagging isn\'t a partner unwilling; they usually just need more lead-in. Letting them watch you do it once is far easier than telling them to try it cold.',
            'likes' => [
                'Toys: bullet vibes, wands, restraints — starting from the entry level.',
                'Positions you haven\'t tried, and places that aren\'t the bed.',
                'Role play and scenario setups.',
                'The list system: each of you writes what you want to try, swap, circle the overlaps.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'In daily life you sign up first and research later — new restaurant, new route, new anything. Your relationships never coast on autopilot; the price is that sometimes they need you to wait up.',
            'deep' => 'Your engine runs on "not yet tried," so the thing your relationship fears most is autopilot. But beware the to-do list trap — always chasing the next item means nothing gets fully experienced, and your partner starts feeling like an item being checked off. Deliberately repeat something you loved once in a while; repetition grows its own new layers. And when they can\'t keep up, it isn\'t unwillingness — they usually need more on-ramp: talk first, watch first, try it once under the safest possible conditions.',
            'strength' => 'You keep the relationship permanently off autopilot — there is always a next item, a next possibility. People with you rarely feel bored, and that alone is rare.',
            'watch' => 'Don\'t let exploring become a checklist. Racing item to item, nothing gets fully experienced — and your partner starts to feel like a box being ticked.',
            'match' => 'Best click with the "Trainer" — they know how to take you through it safely. Another "Explorer" means an endless shared list to tick together.',
            'friction' => 'The "Guardian" and the "Devotee" take work: your "let\'s try something new" reads as restlessness or dissatisfaction. Say it plainly — novelty doesn\'t conflict with loyalty, or with safety.',
            'partner_line' => '"I love trying new things — not because now isn\'t good enough, but because unlocking things with you is thrilling. If I\'m going too fast, say so. We slow down; we don\'t give up."',
        ],

        'ritual' => [
            'name' => 'Ritualist', 'slug' => 'ritual', 'tag' => 'Free inside the frame',
            'line' => 'You like rules and a flow. What\'s been agreed on is where you let go the most.',
            'long' => 'A fixed opening, a fixed order — that\'s not boring to you, it\'s safe. Knowing what happens next is what frees you to actually feel it. A last-minute change of script pulls you out, and the "surprise" lands as an interruption, not a bonus.',
            'signals' => [
                'A familiar opening gets you into it faster.',
                'A last-minute change of plan pulls you right out.',
                'You ask "what are we doing next?" — and you genuinely want the answer.',
                '"Surprises" usually land on you as interruptions, not gifts.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'Structure isn\'t boring to you — it\'s what frees your attention to feel: knowing what comes next — shower first, ten minutes of kissing, whose mouth goes first, how it ends — means none of your attention is spent predicting. So the fixed opening, the agreed order, the agreed signals actually let you go further than anyone: once the frame is set, you can take however heavy and however long it gets inside it. This gets misread as rigidity, but what you want is a predictable frame, not predictable content. Say that clearly and they\'ll stop trying to surprise you.',
            'likes' => [
                'A fixed opening: the shower, the lighting, the same stretch of music.',
                'An agreed flow and agreed signals.',
                'A fixed order of positions.',
                'The same closing ritual afterwards, every time.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'Your daily life runs on process too: the fixed route, the fixed order, the fixed seat. That\'s not rigidity — it\'s freeing attention from prediction and saving it for what actually deserves feeling.',
            'deep' => 'Structure isn\'t boredom to you — it\'s what buys you the attention to feel: know what\'s coming, and no bandwidth is wasted predicting it. This gets misread as rigidity, when what you actually want is a predictable frame — inside it you\'re up for anything, and usually bolder than you look. Explain that to your partner and they\'ll stop engineering "surprises," each of which was pulling you out of the moment. If something new must come in, slot it into the same position in the familiar flow — you\'ll receive it just fine.',
            'strength' => 'You trade predictability for depth — with the frame steady, all of your attention lands on sensation. Your intimacy has a stability and a depth most people never reach.',
            'watch' => 'You want a frame, not a cage — but partners can\'t always tell the difference. Leave it unexplained and they\'ll keep gifting you "surprises," each one pulling you out of the moment.',
            'match' => 'Best with the "Sensualist" and the "Guardian" — one treasures the build-up like you do, the other treasures the agreed frame. Your rhythms align by default.',
            'friction' => 'The "Spark" and the "Explorer" need the most negotiation: they run on surprise and novelty, you on process and familiarity. The compromise — keep the frame fixed, and leave one slot inside it for them to improvise.',
            'partner_line' => '"I like having a flow — not because I\'m boring, but because knowing what\'s next is what lets me truly relax and feel it. So don\'t rush to surprise me. Give me familiar first."',
        ],
        'guardian' => [
            'name' => 'Guardian', 'slug' => 'guardian', 'tag' => 'Boundaries come first',
            'line' => 'Boundaries get stated first. You\'re not prudish — you know exactly what you want.',
            'long' => 'Before anything starts, you make the no-list explicit — where not to touch, what\'s off today, which word means stop — and you don\'t find that a buzzkill. It actually frees you: with the lines drawn, the space inside them is real freedom, and inside it you\'re usually bolder than you look.',
            'signals' => [
                'Before starting, you spell out what\'s not happening.',
                'Unagreed spur-of-the-moment ideas hit your brakes first.',
                'Once it\'s all been said clearly, you actually play harder.',
                'When someone calls you difficult or says you "ruined the mood" — you quietly file that away.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'You draw the lines before you begin: where\'s off-limits, what\'s not happening (no anal, no marks, no filming), which word ends everything instantly. That\'s not a buzzkill — it\'s pouring the foundation. With boundaries clear, the inside is real freedom, and inside them you\'re often far bolder than you look: what\'s been agreed to, you commit to completely. You\'ll notice the people happy to work with this are usually the ones most worth going deeper with; anyone who calls you difficult or says you "ruined the mood" was never a fit to begin with.',
            'likes' => [
                'The list beforehand: what\'s a yes, what\'s a no, what\'s a not-today.',
                'A safeword — one that actually gets used.',
                'Trying anything new under the safest conditions first.',
                'The next-day debrief, then deciding together whether to level up.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'In daily life you read the rules first and ask questions early: contracts get finished, itineraries get confirmed. It makes you hard to railroad — and it means the people who\'ll happily work within your lines are usually the ones truly worth going deeper with.',
            'deep' => 'Spelling out the no-list first is the most underrated skill on this whole site. You\'re not killing the mood — you\'re pouring the foundation. And you\'ll find that people willing to work with it are generally the ones worth going deeper with; the ones who call you difficult never fit anyway. One caution: don\'t treat the list as final. Boundaries move. Renegotiate every so often and the territory you can play in turns out far larger than you thought.',
            'strength' => 'You pour the foundation first — clear lines are what make the inside truly free. It\'s the most underrated skill there is, and the very thing that lets a partner genuinely let go.',
            'watch' => 'Your care can read as buzzkill to the impatient. Don\'t shrink because of them: whoever finds you difficult never fit; whoever works with you is the one worth going deeper with.',
            'match' => 'Steadiest with the "Ritualist" and the "Trainer" — one values agreed rules like you do, the other knows how to take you far while staying inside your lines.',
            'friction' => 'The "Explorer" and the "Exhibitionist" need extra communication: they push outward, you need the range fixed first. The trick: frame boundaries as the "list of what we can play with," not the "list of what\'s forbidden."',
            'partner_line' => '"I state my boundaries first not because I\'m prudish, but so we can truly let go inside them — get the no-list clear, and everything we do want gets played to the hilt."',
        ],
        'spark' => [
            'name' => 'Spark', 'slug' => 'spark', 'tag' => 'Zero to a hundred in seconds',
            'line' => 'Zero to a hundred takes you three seconds. Anything scheduled is missing something.',
            'long' => 'What you want is suddenness: starting the moment the door closes, clothes half-off, at exactly the wrong hour. A booked time and place feels like completing a task — what actually gets you is "we weren\'t planning to, and then it happened."',
            'signals' => [
                'Zero to a hundred in three seconds — no warm-up required.',
                '"We weren\'t planning to, and then we did" is precisely your flavor.',
                'Scheduled, you go cold. Ambushed, you don\'t.',
                'You\'re usually the one saying "now — right now."',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'What you want is suddenness: pressed against the wall inside the front door, starting with your pants still caught around your legs, in the kitchen, while they\'re mid-phone-call. A booked time and place feels like ticking off a chore; you need the accident — no shower, nothing prepared, only ten minutes — and it works better. But a long-term relationship can\'t live on chance alone: the moment life gets busy, the gaps where things "just happen" are the first to vanish. When that comes, the planned night isn\'t a compromise, it\'s another way to play — your "sudden" as the opening move, then hand it to your partner to stretch out the rest.',
            'likes' => [
                'The unannounced approach — straight to it.',
                'Clothes half-on, over fast — that kind.',
                'A change of venue — anywhere that isn\'t the bed.',
                'Daytime, right before leaving, those first minutes home.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'You improvise through daily life too: leave on a whim, change plans mid-stride. That summon-it-from-nowhere energy is magnetic — but when life gets busy, the gaps where things "just happen" vanish first, and you\'ll feel the loss keenly.',
            'deep' => 'What you want is "we weren\'t planning to, and then it happened." That can\'t be scheduled — but its conditions can be created: unscheduled hours, an afternoon with nothing booked, an approach with no warning. The flip side: long-term, chance alone isn\'t enough — busy seasons erase the accidental gaps first, and you\'ll feel something\'s missing before anyone else does. That\'s when you allow the planned one, treated as a different game rather than a concession: your suddenness as the cold open, the rest handed over to be drawn out.',
            'strength' => 'You have a catch-them-off-guard magnetism — arriving fully lit, no warm-up. That improvised charge can ignite a room in seconds, and most people couldn\'t buy it if they tried.',
            'watch' => 'Long-term, chance alone won\'t feed you. Life gets busy and the "just happened" gaps close first — that\'s when the planned night is a continuation, not a concession.',
            'match' => 'Best flavor match is the "Explorer" — you both love the accident, the unannounced next thing. Real sparks with the "Brat" too: you both run on improvisation.',
            'friction' => 'The "Slow Burn" and the "Ritualist" are your biggest rhythm gaps: you want the instant, they need the build. Compromise — your "sudden" opens the show, then they stretch the rest out long.',
            'partner_line' => '"What I want is that \'we weren\'t planning to — and then it happened\' feeling. You don\'t have to arrange a thing: one unannounced move toward me is the best opening there is."',
        ],
        'slowburn' => [
            'name' => 'Slow Burn', 'slug' => 'slow-burn', 'tag' => 'Builds layer by layer',
            'line' => 'What you need is layer on layer. Too fast and the thread just snaps.',
            'long' => 'Your rhythm climbs — it doesn\'t leap. Lay the groundwork long enough and the payoff has somewhere to land; rushed, hurried, foreplay skipped, you go completely cold — not from not wanting it, but because the rhythm was cut.',
            'signals' => [
                'It takes twenty minutes before you truly start to feel it.',
                'Foreplay too short, and the whole night never gets off the ground.',
                'Rushing snaps the thread — and it\'s very hard to splice back.',
                'They start teasing you at noon, and you spend the whole afternoon looking forward to it.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'Your rhythm stacks upward — it doesn\'t skip steps: kissed long enough, touched long enough, and the later part has something to land on; not enough groundwork and straight in means pain, dryness, total shutdown. That\'s not disinterest — the rhythm snapped. What works on you isn\'t technique, it\'s time; and when there genuinely isn\'t enough, don\'t compress — split the opening act instead: texts through the day, the first sparks lit early, continued at night. Split build-up works on you just as well — better, even, because by then you\'ve been thinking about it all day.',
            'likes' => [
                'Very long foreplay — long enough they think it isn\'t happening tonight.',
                'In installments: teased through the day, continued at night.',
                'Layer on layer, no skipped steps.',
                'Slow, unhurried, extended mouth and hands.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'You warm to people slowly too: cool at first meeting, most devoted once familiar. Your relationships don\'t detonate — they accumulate, which is why the people who\'ve stayed longest tend to understand you best.',
            'deep' => 'Your rhythm stacks; rushing snaps it, and a snapped thread rarely splices back the same night. Which means what works on you isn\'t technique but time — and the hard part is that real life rarely has enough. Rather than force-compressing, split the opening: start at noon, once more in the evening, close at night. You\'ll find distributed build-up works as well or better. And say "I run slow" upfront — otherwise slow-burn gets misread as disinterest, they start pushing harder, and pushing only slows you further.',
            'strength' => 'You can stack tension higher than anyone — where others peak in three minutes, you can draw one thing across an entire evening. Whoever learns to wait for you gets the strongest pour.',
            'watch' => 'Rushing snaps the thread, and it doesn\'t splice back easily. Time beats technique with you — when time is short, split the build-up across the day rather than compressing it.',
            'match' => 'Best with the "Sensualist" and the "Romantic" — one savors every inch of the process like you do, the other needs the groundwork just as much. None of you are in a hurry.',
            'friction' => 'The "Spark" is your polar opposite in tempo: three seconds versus an hour. The key to coexisting — declare your "slow" in advance, so it never gets misread as not wanting them.',
            'partner_line' => '"I need it built layer by layer — rushing snaps me out of it. That\'s not disinterest; it\'s my rhythm. Stay with me through the long opening, and the finish will be richer than you can imagine."',
        ],
        'aftercare' => [
            'name' => 'Cuddler', 'slug' => 'aftercare', 'tag' => 'No wind-down, no ending',
            'line' => 'The part after matters most to you. No wind-down means it never ended.',
            'long' => 'To you, the ten minutes of holding each other and talking matter as much as everything before — sometimes more. Skip them and the best night still ends up with a hole in it. That isn\'t clinginess. It\'s how you complete a thing.',
            'signals' => [
                'The ten minutes after matter to you as much as the act.',
                'What you want is holding and talking — not two people on separate phones.',
                'Without the wind-down, the best night still feels like something\'s missing.',
                'On a bad day, what you need is to be held — not cheered up.',
            ],
            'bedroom' => 'To you the wind-down is part of the content: being held afterwards, a hand in your hair, one quiet sentence — those ten minutes decide whether the whole thing feels complete. If they get up to shower the moment it ends, or reach for the phone, then no matter how good the sex was, a piece of it goes hollow. That isn\'t clinginess — your emotions need a place to land. Say it out loud: plenty of people believe the orgasm is the ending and honestly don\'t know that part counts. Say it once and they can usually deliver — wiping you down, pulling up the blanket, holding you without a word all qualify.',
            'likes' => [
                'Ten-plus minutes of holding and skin contact after.',
                'Showering together, drying each other off.',
                'A quiet sentence, a hand through your hair, after.',
                'Falling asleep pressed together — no rolling away.',
            ],
            'everyday' => 'In relationships you care about how things end: conversations get finished, anger gets settled, there\'s a hug before anyone leaves. That habit of closing well gives feelings a place to land — and it\'s quietly how many relationships grow deep.',
            'deep' => 'The ten minutes after aren\'t an add-on for you — they\'re the completion. Missing, they hollow out even the best night. That isn\'t clinginess: your emotions need somewhere to land, and right after is when a person is most undefended — the quality of that stretch decides how you remember the whole thing. Say it explicitly: most people genuinely don\'t know the wind-down counts as content, and think the ending is the end. Said once, they can usually deliver. And the heavier the play, the less optional it becomes.',
            'strength' => 'You know how to complete intimacy rather than merely stop it. Those ten minutes give feelings a place to land — it\'s the quiet mechanism by which relationships deepen.',
            'watch' => 'Most people think the end is the end — nobody told them the wind-down is content too. Your silence isn\'t met with refusal, just ignorance. Say it once; that\'s usually all it takes.',
            'match' => 'Warmest with the "Caregiver" and the "Devotee" — one is born wanting to care for you to the very last minute; the other treasures that private after-hour exactly as much as you do.',
            'friction' => 'Watch the "Spark": arrives fast, may finish fast too. Tell them plainly — those ten minutes aren\'t clinginess; they\'re how I finish what we started.',
            'partner_line' => '"The ten minutes you hold me and talk after — they matter to me as much as everything before. It\'s not clinginess; it\'s how I let the whole thing land. Save me a little time, okay?"',
        ],
    ],

